# No Em Dashes or En Dashes

Never write an em dash (U+2014) or an en dash (U+2013). This applies to
everything: code, comments, doc comments, user-facing strings, commit messages,
Markdown, rule files, and chat replies.

This file deliberately names the characters by code point rather than printing
them, so that `grep -rIn '[\u2014\u2013]'` over the project stays empty.

Use ordinary punctuation instead, chosen to fit the sentence:

- Parenthetical aside: a comma pair or parentheses.
- Introducing an explanation or a list: a colon.
- Joining two independent clauses: a semicolon, or split into two sentences.
- Numeric and date ranges: a plain hyphen, or the word "to".
- A "no value" placeholder in the UI: a plain hyphen.

```dart
// BAD: reads "not discarded \u2014 it goes to the log"
/// Technical detail is not discarded \u2014 it goes to the log.
return value == null ? '\u2014' : format(value);
Text('$from \u2013 $to');

// GOOD
/// Technical detail is not discarded: it goes to the log.
return value == null ? '-' : format(value);
Text('$from to $to');
```

Hyphens in compound words (`mobile-first`, `clock-in`) are fine, as are `·`
separators and `…` ellipses. Only the two long dashes are banned.
